# Architecture Standards

**WORKSPACE RULE - ALWAYS APPLY**

## Purpose
Maintain consistent folder structure and architectural patterns

## Folder Structure Standards

### Next.js App Router Structure
```
src/app/
├── admin/           # Admin-only pages
├── api/             # API routes
├── auth/            # Authentication pages  
├── manuscripts/     # Core feature pages
└── (other features)/
```

### Component Organization
```
src/components/
├── [FeatureName].tsx    # Main feature components
├── [FeatureName]Form.tsx # Form components
├── [FeatureName]List.tsx # List components
└── [Shared].tsx         # Shared utilities
```

### API Route Patterns
```
src/app/api/
├── admin/          # Admin-only endpoints
├── auth/           # Authentication
├── [resource]/     # CRUD endpoints
│   ├── route.ts    # GET, POST
│   └── [id]/
│       └── route.ts # GET, PUT, DELETE
```

## Database Architecture

### Prisma Schema Organization
- Core models first (User, Manuscript)
- Related models grouped together
- Enums at bottom
- Consistent naming (PascalCase models, camelCase fields)

### Migration Standards
- Descriptive migration names
- Test migrations locally first
- Document breaking changes

## Security Architecture

### Authentication Flow
- NextAuth.js for session management
- Role-based access control
- Invitation-only registration

### API Security
- Session validation on protected routes
- Role checking for admin endpoints
- Input validation with Zod

## Performance Standards

### Database Optimization
- Indexes on frequently queried fields
- Cascade deletions for data integrity
- Pagination for large datasets

### Frontend Optimization
- Server components by default
- Client components only when needed
- Proper loading states

## File Naming Conventions

### Pages
- `page.tsx` for route pages
- `layout.tsx` for layouts
- `loading.tsx` for loading states
- `error.tsx` for error boundaries

### Components
- PascalCase for component files
- Descriptive names (ManuscriptForm vs Form)
- Group related components in folders

### API Routes
- `route.ts` for API endpoints
- RESTful naming conventions
- Consistent HTTP methods

## Code Organization Rules

### Import Order
1. React/Next.js imports
2. Third-party libraries
3. Internal utilities
4. Components
5. Types/interfaces

### Component Structure
1. Interfaces/types at top
2. Main component function
3. Helper functions at bottom
4. Export at bottom

## Enforcement
- Follow existing patterns when adding features
- Maintain consistent naming across similar files
- Group related functionality together
- Keep components focused and single-purpose